{:edb/db-before-t 657 :edb/db-after-t 658 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533970 50 #inst "2026-10-05T20:27:08.756Z" 13194139533970 true] [17592186092165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275351 13194139533970 true] [17592186092165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275358 13194139533970 true] [17592186092166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275365 13194139533970 true] [17592186092166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275370 13194139533970 true] [17592186092218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275344 13194139533970 true] [17592186275344 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275345 13194139533970 true] [17592186275344 1089 "q-120598" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275344 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533970 true] [17592186275344 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/assets/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275344 1113 #uuid "1b42d321-a6dc-55db-8590-3f5b61b3f660" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275344 1145 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275346 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275347 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275348 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275349 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275350 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1041 "selection" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275348 13194139533970 true] [17592186275345 1110 #uuid "e93a35a2-dee9-5eb3-8be4-766470363a67" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275346 1034 "(2,2)" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533970 true] [17592186275346 1111 #uuid "bd3bdcff-cafd-5c81-9906-5fa096f213fd" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275347 1034 "(1,1)" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533970 true] [17592186275347 1111 #uuid "ecede080-4c7e-50bc-bde9-67966e01760d" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275348 1034 "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533970 true] [17592186275348 1111 #uuid "b65bed6c-449e-5eda-b62e-d9f0f915a7ee" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275349 1034 "(\\sqrt{2},2)" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275349 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533970 true] [17592186275349 1111 #uuid "748ab3ce-ba90-5c75-9eba-5e0dfc1c4227" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275350 1034 "(2,\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533970 true] [17592186275350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533970 true] [17592186275350 1111 #uuid "25054c41-b8ad-5fd